# Normalize everything to LF, in the repository *and* in the working tree.
#
# Without the `eol=lf`, a Windows clone with the default `core.autocrlf=true`
# checks Go files out with CRLF line endings. `gofmt` then reports every single
# file as "not properly formatted", so `make lint` fails locally on 90+ files
# that CI, running on Linux, considers clean. That is a full day lost to a
# non-problem, so the working tree is pinned rather than left to a global setting.
#
# This does not rewrite history: the committed blobs are already LF, and adding
# this file shows no diff, because git normalizes on the way in either way. What
# it does not do by itself is rewrite a working tree that was already checked out
# with CRLF. To convert one, from a clean tree (commit or stash first -- the
# reset discards uncommitted work):
#
#     git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard
#
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg -text
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.exe  binary
*.gz   binary
*.zip  binary

# Generated Go code. Marking it linguist-generated collapses it in pull request
# diffs, which is the whole point: nobody reviews deepcopy methods by hand.
**/zz_generated.*.go linguist-generated=true

# Generated Kubernetes manifests. Still reviewed (a CRD change matters), so they
# are not marked generated -- but they are committed artifacts, see the Makefile.
deploy/install.yaml linguist-generated=true
